    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d382">

                <pb facs="0423" n="411" xml:id="pg411"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00112a European War, 1939/29263:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Argentina</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Armour</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Buenos
                                Aires</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1943-04-21T21:00:00-03:00">April 21, 1943—9
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 9:50 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>878. Reference Department’s telegram 570, April 20, 6 p.m.<anchor n="75"
                        xml:id="d382-f1"/> Embassy believes information contained despatch 7388,
                    November 18<anchor n="76" xml:id="d382-f2"/> sufficient to approach Argentine
                    Ambassador. Situation today approximately same. However, Embassy is attempting
                    to obtain additional information.</p>
                <p><hi rendition="#italic">El Pampero, Deutsche La Plata Zeitung, Editorial
                        Argentina, II Mattino D’ltalia</hi> as well as pro-totalitarian <hi
                        rendition="#italic">El Momento Argentino</hi> and <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Cabildo</hi> are all receiving newsprint from Ministry of Agriculture. This
                    Ministry receives, according best information available, approximately 300 tons
                    locally manufactured newsprint, paying from 65 to 70 centavos per kilo which is
                    below free market price for locally manufactured newsprint, thus in addition to
                    supplying Government grants slight subsidy.</p>
                <p>Before making presentation to Argentine Ambassador the Department will
                    undoubtedly take into account the fact that this case is one of the most telling
                    points in report contemplated under section 5(<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>) of
                    policy memorandum of March 4.<anchor n="77" xml:id="d382-f3"/> That report
                    should be ready for presentation by June 1.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Armour</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d382-f1" n="75" xml:id="d382-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; this telegram requested that reply be expedited to Department’s
                    telegram No. 437 of March 29, 1943, 3 p.m., which requested information on the
                    distribution to pro-Axis papers of newsprint made from Swedish pulp (740.00112A
                    European War 1939/21092).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d382-f2" n="76" xml:id="d382-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed; this despatch reported that the Argentine Minister of Agriculture
                    bought newsprint locally and distributed it to pro-Axis papers (740.00112A
                    European War 1939/21092).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d382-f3" n="77" xml:id="d382-f3-content">
                <p><hi rendition="#italic">Post</hi>, <ref target="#pg471">p. 471</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
